<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      REQUIRED IN main, not just debug/profile. Flutter's template adds INTERNET only to the
      debug and profile manifests, so a RELEASE build ships with no network permission at all:
      Supabase.initialize() throws and the process dies before the first frame. The app appears
      to "not open". Every screen in Nivora needs the network, so it belongs here.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!-- Lets the app tell "you are offline" apart from "the server is down". -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!-- ── WHAT IS ASKED FOR AT RUNTIME, AND WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT ──────────────────────

         POST_NOTIFICATIONS is a runtime permission from Android 13 (API 33). Without it the app
         can hold a valid FCM token, the server can send a perfectly good message, and NOTHING
         APPEARS — silently. Rent reminders, a notice from the owner and "payment received" all
         arrive through it.

         WHEN IT IS ASKED FOR: the first time a session appears, not on first launch. The token
         is registered against a USER, so there is nothing to register before somebody signs in,
         and asking a stranger on the sign-in screen is asking before the answer can mean
         anything. There is no in-app rationale screen ahead of the system dialog — the dialog
         is the ask — and a refusal is not treated as an error: the token is registered anyway,
         so turning notifications on later in Settings works immediately. See
         lib/core/notify/push_service.dart.

         CAMERA is declared because a warden photographs an ID proof and a resident photographs a
         complaint. It is the one dangerous permission this app holds, and DECLARING IT IS WHAT
         MAKES IT MANDATORY: ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE throws SecurityException for an app that
         declares CAMERA without holding it, so data/capture.dart asks first. The uses-feature
         line keeps the app installable on a device with no camera — a declared permission
         otherwise implies required hardware and quietly filters the store listing.

         NOT DECLARED, each absence deliberate:
           READ_MEDIA_IMAGES / READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE — the gallery goes through Android's photo
             picker, which returns one chosen image and needs no permission at all. Asking for the
             media library would trigger Play's Photo and Video Permissions declaration for a
             capability this app does not use.
           READ_CONTACTS — nothing in Nivora reads a contact. A guardian's phone is typed into a
             form by the warden.
           AD_ID — no advertising, no analytics SDK, no ad id. Removed EXPLICITLY rather than
             merely left out: a dependency's manifest can merge it in, and its presence would
             contradict the Data safety declaration. tools:node="remove" makes that a fact about
             the merged manifest instead of a hope. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <uses-permission android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.AD_ID" tools:node="remove" />
    <!-- ── THE SESSION MUST NOT LEAVE THE PHONE ────────────────────────────────────────────
         Android Auto Backup is ON BY DEFAULT. With it on,
         /data/data/com.srnivora.app/shared_prefs/FlutterSharedPreferences.xml is uploaded to
         the user's Google Drive and restored by Android 12+ device-to-device transfer — and
         that file holds the persisted GoTrue session, including a long-lived refresh token for
         an account that moves other people's rent.

         A warden who hands on a phone, or restores onto a second handset, would carry a live
         session with them. It is also an off-device transfer of account data that
         docs/data-safety.md does not declare, in a document whose own §1 defines "collected"
         as "transmitted off the user's device".

         Nothing is lost by refusing it. This client keeps no local state worth restoring; every
         screen is server-backed and the cost of a fresh install is signing in once.

         BOTH attributes are needed and they are not duplicates: allowBackup governs cloud
         backup on every version, dataExtractionRules governs cloud backup AND D2D transfer on
         Android 12+ (targetSdk here is 36), and a device transfer is not a "backup". -->
    <application
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              THE EMAIL CONFIRMATION LINK COMES BACK HERE.

              GoTrue matches the one-time token at /auth/v1/verify and then 302s to whatever
              `redirect_to` the app asked for. That destination is this filter, so the link
              opens NIVORA rather than a web page: supabase_flutter's deep-link observer sees
              the `?code=` on the incoming Uri, exchanges it for a session using the PKCE code
              verifier it stored when it asked for the mail, and the person is SIGNED IN BY THE
              LINK. That sign-in is the proof the owner asked for.

              A browser can never complete that exchange - it does not hold the verifier - which
              is why the previous landing-page design could only ever show a page and hope.

              A CUSTOM SCHEME, AND NOT http/https, DELIBERATELY.
              An <data android:scheme="https"> filter would offer this app as a handler for
              ordinary web links and, without a verified Digital Asset Link, Android would show
              a disambiguation sheet on somebody else's URLs. `com.srnivora.app` is this app's
              own applicationId used as a scheme (see android/app/build.gradle.kts), which is
              the reverse-DNS form Android and RFC 3986 both allow and which nothing else on the
              device can claim. The scheme AND the host are both pinned, so the ONLY Uri this
              filter accepts is com.srnivora.app://verify-email[?...]. No http, no https, no
              bare scheme wildcard.

              MUST BE MIRRORED IN THE SUPABASE DASHBOARD or no link ever arrives here: paste

                  com.srnivora.app://verify-email

              into Authentication -> URL Configuration -> Redirect URLs. Measured against this
              project on 2026-09-01: GoTrue does not refuse an unlisted redirect, it SILENTLY
              substitutes the Site URL, so a missing entry looks like a working link that opens
              the wrong thing. docs/email-verification.md has the whole procedure.

              The activity is launchMode="singleTop", so a link tapped while NIVORA is already
              running is delivered to the existing instance via onNewIntent rather than starting
              a second copy of the app.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="com.srnivora.app" android:host="verify-email"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>

        <!--
          UPI APPS MUST BE VISIBLE OR THEY DO NOT EXIST.

          From Android 11 (API 30) — and this app targets 36 — package visibility is filtered by
          default: an app cannot see what else is installed unless it declares what it is looking
          for here. Razorpay's checkout builds its "Pay using GPay / PhonePe / Paytm" list by
          resolving this exact intent, so WITHOUT this block that list resolves empty and the
          sheet silently drops UPI intent altogether.

          That is the single most-used way to pay rent in India, and the failure is invisible: no
          crash, no error, no log — just a checkout that quietly offers cards and netbanking to a
          resident who has never owned a card. It also cannot be caught on an older test device,
          because the filtering only applies at API 30+.
        -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
            <data android:scheme="upi"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
